# ~/.near

🟨 .near folder

---

### ~/.near

the .near folder

```sh
~/.near/
├── config.json
├── genesis.json
├── node_key.json
├── validator_key.json
└── data/
```

##### genesis.json

genesis block

##### \*\_key.json

both of the `_key.json` files have the same structure, the "node_key.json" is generated automatically and does not need to be touched, for the "validator_key.json" file you must generate a new key pair and have the account id for your staking .pool.near account

```json
{
	"account_id": "your.pool.near",
	"public_key": "ed25519:public_key",
	"secret_key": "ed25519:secret_key"
}
```

##### data/

data folder

##### config.json

the config.json file, see [near-nodes.io/validator/compile-and-run-a-node#31-set-up-the-config-file-for-your-needs-1](https://near-nodes.io/validator/compile-and-run-a-node#31-set-up-the-config-file-for-your-needs-1)

- [Regular RPC Node](https://s3-us-west-1.amazonaws.com/build.nearprotocol.com/nearcore-deploy/mainnet/rpc/config.json) stores recent info for all the shards, and can serve RPC queries for the last N epochs (N=5 by default).
- [Archival RPC Node](https://s3-us-west-1.amazonaws.com/build.nearprotocol.com/nearcore-deploy/mainnet/archival/config.json) stores all the info starting for genesis, can serve RPC queries for the entire history. Requires significant storage.
- [Validator Node](https://s3-us-west-1.amazonaws.com/build.nearprotocol.com/nearcore-deploy/mainnet/validator/config.json) is designed for the validators. Heavily optimized, does not store the full state, and cannot serve RPC queries.

ℹ️ the S3 files above are a minimal bootable base, not a tuned config: `boot_nodes` ships empty, `state_sync` ships `null` (falls back to conservative code defaults), peer counts are conservative, and as of `neard 2.13.4` the S3 bundle still contains the removed `consensus.block_fetch_horizon` key (node logs a warning and starts anyway). After `neard init`, apply the [boot-nodes](/boot-nodes) script and the tuned values from [config](/config) — or start from the ready-made `static/json/config.rpc.json` / `config.validator.json` in this repo, which are rebased on `neard 2.13.4` output.

⚠️ state sync *delivers* inbound: peers open connections back to you to push state parts. Two things are mandatory or state sync fails forever while headers look fine: (1) forward **TCP 24567 → your node** on your router/NAT, and (2) if behind NAT set `network.experimental.tier3_public_addr` to `"YOUR_PUBLIC_IP:24567"` (verify with `curl localhost:3031/metrics | grep near_tier3_public_addr` after setting `rpc.prometheus_addr`). Without both, the node follows head perfectly and holds zero state — height lies, only queries tell the truth (see below).

ℹ️ **height is necessary, queries are sufficient.** `syncing: false` at head proves header sync only. Verify real state with a view call, e.g. `query(view_account)` for an account on each tracked shard — `UNAVAILABLE_SHARD` means the node has no data for that shard (the message says "does not track" but the code raises it on missing chunk/state data), regardless of what the config lists.

ℹ️ **explicit `tracked_shards_config: {"Shards": [...]}` cannot bootstrap state from scratch** (verified live on `neard 2.13.4`): shard matching needs epoch-manager lookups, lookup errors are masked as "not tracked", the tracking set resolves empty, and state sync "completes" instantly with zero work (`tracking_shards=[]` in debug logs). Workaround: sync with `"AllShards"` first (it skips the lookups), then narrow to an explicit list *after* `syncing: false` + passing queries — narrowing a state-holding node is a minutes-long block catch-up.

see [config.md]({ROUTES.config.path}), for json schema for config.json

